  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nOur Labor Day is off to a quiet start. Temperatures are slowly \nfalling through the 60s and blowoff cirrus is scooting overhead \nfrom decaying convection to the west. There is a signal for some\npatches of fog to develop south of I-80 as well as west of the \nFox River valley toward daybreak which seems reasonable since  \ndewpoint depressions are nearing zero across a number of sites \nearly this morning in these areas. Accordingly, added some \npatchy fog to the morning forecast over these areas. Otherwise, \nweather looks seasonably pleasant for the holiday today with \nhighs in the lower 80s away from the lake and middle to upper \n70s closer to the lakeshore, coolest near the Illinois \nlakeshore. Also looking at plenty of sunshine filtered by \npassing high level clouds and a bit of an E breeze.\n\nThe ridge axis will pass over tonight turning the wind field to\nsoutherly which will promote a bit of a warmup for Tuesday with\nhighs forecast in the middle 80s to near 90, including up to \nthe lake with the offshore flow.\n\nImmediately in the wake of the ridge, a deepening upper low \nwill track west to east across southern Canada during the middle\npart of the week and drag a surface cold front across the area \non Wednesday bringing our next opportunity for rain and \nthunderstorms. Models have come into much better alignment \nregarding this system over the past 24 hours. The main window \nfor precip looks to be from late Tuesday night into Wednesday \nafternoon as the front moves into our northwest, during what is\nnot typically a diurnally favorable window for thunderstorm \ndevelopment. Overall, convective parameter spacing during this \ntime is marginal with modest, largely capped instability ahead \nof the front and the much better forcing and shear staying \nwrapped up in the circulation to our north. Efficient warm \nadvection and moist upglide ahead of the front may help \nconvection get going, particularly through the morning hours. \nGeneral expectation is a southeastward-moving line of  \nscattered to widespread showers with some embedded thunder \ncoverage, highest favored during the earlier half of the day\nWednesday. The marginal severe potential we'd been eyeing is \nbeginning to look like a bit of a stretch. The better dynamic \nand kinematic environments will lag well behind the surface \nfront into Wednesday and won't catch up until Wednesday night or\nearly Thursday as the upper trough axis encroaches. By this \ntime, the front should be parked across central IL and rain \nchances Wednesday night into Thursday should be confined to our \nfar south, if not out of our CWA altogether. SPC's new Day 3 \nfeatures a marginal risk across central IL abutting our southern\nCWA for later Wednesday night.\n\nHigh pressure will then overspread the region late in the week \nbeneath the windward side of the departing trough. Seasonably \nwarm conditions will continue through the end of the week. A \nnumber of subtle disturbances tracking overhead may drive \nperiodic chances for rain over the weekend. Medium range \nguidance continues to favor a larger synoptic system bringing \nrain to the region in the late Sunday to Monday period.\n\nDoom",
